peng Wang , cite17†Xin Dai , cite18†Liang Wang , cite19†Chin-Chia Michael Yeh , cite20†Yujie Fan , cite21†Yan Zheng , cite22†Vineeth Rakesh , cite23†Huiyuan Chen , cite24†Guanchu Wang , cite25†Mangesh Bendre , cite26†Zhongfang Zhuang , cite27†Xiaoting Li , cite28†Prince Aboagye , cite29†Vivian Lai , cite30†Minghua Xu , cite31†Hao Yang , cite32†Yiwei Cai , cite33†Mahashweta Das , cite34†Yuzhong Chen L11: View a PDF of the paper titled TransactionGPT, by Yingtong Dou and 28 other authors\nL12: \nL13: cite35†View PDF cite36†HTML (experimental) L14: > Abstract:We present TransactionGPT (TGPT), a foundation model for consumer transaction data within one of the world's largest payment networks. TGPT is designed to understand and generate transaction trajectories while simultaneously supporting a variety of downstream prediction and classification tasks. We introduce a novel 3D-Transformer architecture specifically tailored for capturing the complex dynamics in payment transaction data.\nL15: This architecture incorporates design innovations that enhance modality fusion and computational efficiency, while seamlessly enabling joint optimization with downstream objectives. Trained on billion-scale real-world transactions, TGPT significantly improves downstream anomaly transaction detection performance against a competitive production model and exhibits advantages over baselines in generating future transactions.\nL16: We conduct extensive empirical evaluations utilizing a diverse collection of company transaction datasets spanning multiple downstream tasks, thereby enabling a thorough assessment of TGPT's effectiveness and efficiency in comparison to established methodologies. Furthermore, we examine the incorporation of LLM-derived embeddings within TGPT and benchmark its performance against fine-tuned LLMs, demonstrating that TGPT achieves superior predictive accuracy as well as faster training and inference.\nL17: We anticipate that the architectural innovations and practical guidelines from this work will advance foundation models for transaction-like data and catalyze future research in this emerging field.\nL18: Comments:  | Technical Report\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite37†arXiv:2511.08939 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite38†arXiv:2511.08939v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite39†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08939†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Yingtong Dou [cite40†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 03:31:34 UTC (4,140 KB)\nL27: [v2] Mon, 2 Mar 2026 22:48:45 UTC (4,082 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled TransactionGPT, by Yingtong Dou and 28 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite35†View PDF L36:   * cite36†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite41†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite42†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite43†< prev |   cite44†next > L45: \nL46: cite45†new | cite46†recent | cite47†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite48†cs L51: cite49†cs.CL L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite50†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite51†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite52†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite53†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connecte