2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 25 Dec 2025 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Thinking Forward and Backward: Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning for Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Wenda Wei , cite7†Yu-An Liu , cite8†Ruqing Zhang , cite9†Jiafeng Guo , cite10†Lixin Su , cite11†Shuaiqiang Wang , cite12†Dawei Yin , cite13†Maarten de Rijke , cite14†Xueqi Cheng L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Thinking Forward and Backward: Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning for Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning, by Wenda Wei and 8 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has proven to be effective in mitigating hallucinations in large language models, yet its effectiveness remains limited in complex, multi-step reasoning scenarios. Recent efforts have incorporated search-based interactions into RAG, enabling iterative reasoning with real-time retrieval. Most approaches rely on outcome-based supervision, offering no explicit guidance for intermediate steps. This often leads to reward hacking and degraded response quality.\nL17: We propose Bi-RAR, a novel retrieval-augmented reasoning framework that evaluates each intermediate step jointly in both forward and backward directions. To assess the information completeness of each step, we introduce a bidirectional information distance grounded in Kolmogorov complexity, approximated via language model generation probabilities. This quantification measures both how far the current reasoning is from the answer and how well it addresses the question.\nL18: To optimize reasoning under these bidirectional signals, we adopt a multi-objective reinforcement learning framework with a cascading reward structure that emphasizes early trajectory alignment. Empirical results on seven question answering benchmarks demonstrate that Bi-RAR surpasses previous methods and enables efficient interaction and reasoning with the search engine during training and inference.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2511.09109 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2511.09109v3 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09109†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Wenda Wei [cite20†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 08:29:39 UTC (303 KB)\nL27: cite21†[v2] Thu, 13 Nov 2025 06:08:52 UTC (303 KB)\nL28: [v3] Thu, 25 Dec 2025 08:50:33 UTC (294 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Thinking Forward and Backward: Multi-Objective Reinforcement Learning for Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning, by Wenda Wei and 8 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite15†View PDF L37:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite22†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite23†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L46: \nL47: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite29†cs L52: cite30†cs.AI L53: cite31†cs.IR L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite32†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite33†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite34†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (cite35†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Connecte